### Method reminder

Iron filings become tiny magnets in a magnetic field and line up along the field lines. A plotting compass shows the direction, because the north-seeking end of its needle points along the field.

### Solution

(a) Place the bar magnet on a flat table and lay a sheet of white paper or card over it. Sprinkle iron filings evenly on the card, then tap the card gently so the filings can move. They set themselves along curved lines that join the two poles; the pattern is the shape of the magnetic field.

(b) Any three of:

- the lines are smooth closed curves that leave the north pole and enter the south pole;
- the lines are closest together at the poles, where the field is strongest;
- the lines get further apart away from the magnet, where the field is weaker;
- the lines never cross each other;
- the pattern is symmetrical about the axis and about the middle of the magnet.

(c) Use a **plotting compass**. Place it near the magnet and mark the positions of both ends of the needle. Move the compass so that its south end sits where its north end was, and mark again. Joining the marks gives a field line, and the north-seeking end of the needle shows the direction: away from the north pole and towards the south pole of the magnet.
